augmented reality helps people  experience digital information in a new  way  join us as we talk to torch about how  they're enabling anyone to build a our  experiences on this episode of snapchat  thanks for joining us Josh tell us a  little bit more about what torch does  thanks for having me mark torch is an AR  design and publishing platform so we  we're a mobile first workflow for  building AR inside of a are we let  designers very quickly prototype and  develop augmented reality experiences  that they can then share with other  people so what does the architecture  look like for people who are going to go  in and then build an AR app so we had  three main goals when building torch one  auto we wanted the camera first mobile  first workflow we wanted to allow people  to easily bring in whatever assets they  have whether it's images video 3d models  all that and then we wanted to enable  collaboration and sort of semi real-time  collaboration between users on the  platform so we went with firebase for  the mobile device portion of that  because it allowed us to not really pay  attention to a lot of what we needed to  do to offload computation into the cloud  you know working on a mobile device  we're already very limited with CPU and  GPU we're running computer vision to do  AR we're running real-time 3d rendering  at 60 frames a second so we needed to be  able to shift some of that computation  in the end of the cloud and firebase  lets us do that really easily by calling  cloud functions by using real-time  databases things like that for asset  import we wanted to be able to support  all sorts of file types and to do it in  a very fast and efficient manner and so  we did that through a combination of  firebase functions and gke so rather  than trying to do any kind of  computational expensive processing on  the mobile device we ship assets to the  cloud run them through a cloud processor  in JK and then ship them back into  functions where we then an update fire  store and that lets the mobile device  know that every  everything is ready some examples of  what we do in the cloud are encoding  images into GPU formats we do model  conversions between various 3d file  formats we process video for real time  things like that and then for  collaboration we are really leaning  heavily on the firestore so we use  real-time snapshotting in order to let  multiple clients be writing to the same  set of documents and in real time update  everything that's happening as as it's  happening we do this a little bit  differently than you might think so  we're not using single documents as a  document store we're doing time series  data and what's also known as events or  saying where every event has ever  happened in a project we know and we  track and then to build up the final  result you run through every event and  generate the full scene document the  full project document and then for  performance and bandwidth reasons every  once in a while in a cloud function we  will what we call checkpoint those  events and so that takes those events  that up until a certain time turns it  into the final project format and so  then to retrieve a project we retrieve  the latest checkpoint and any event that  has happened since so it sounds like a  lot of the real-time collaboration of  course depends on fire store what made  you choose fire store is the right way  to handle this so we had two main  reasons for that because we're primarily  a mobile app and we didn't want to write  any cloud infrastructure that we didn't  really need to direct access to the DB  from mobile and from cloud functions was  it was really important to us on the  other side of that that's a really  double-edged sword because it means that  you need to be really careful about your  schema management and and making sure  that you're not doing anything bad on  either side of those and so what we  ended up doing is building a type safe  layer in typescript for access to the  database that makes sure that you know  programmers are not willy-nilly  schema changing things when we don't  know about it and then we've also added  the ability to version a document and  migrate between between versions so the  second reason we chose firestore is due  to that snapshotting that i talked about  earlier where we can just set up  callbacks and get live data as things  change and it's just really easy to hook  into I took into application state and  so it just it provides us with a  programming model that makes all that  early easy so it sounds like you may  have made some changes over time if you  could go back and re architect this  based on what you learned over the  process what would you do differently so  the first thing is if we would redo it  today cabrón now exists and so a lot of  what we actually built up in gke to the  you asset processing we could do without  having to build what we built in GTA and  so we would probably move as much of our  as much of the code that is in gke and  Cod functions into cabrón  but it didn't exist at the time so oh  well  the second thing is schema management  the schema that we first chose for  assets made it very expensive for us to  determine does a user have access to  this asset and so what we've ended up  with is in order to securely store  assets but also make it very cheap for  users for clients to access any  information they need to download an  asset  we we do real-time syncing of asset data  into the project so any any information  that a client needs in order to recreate  the project gets mirrored into the  project thanks for telling us about how  you actually built this application to  help other people build their own  applications absolutely thanks for  having me  get started with cloud firestore by  checking out this Quick Start Guide  thanks for watching and make sure to  subscribe for more great Google cloud  platform content we'll see you next time  on stack check
 

